#{[17592186338887 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(-1)=\\lim_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)=8.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{f(-1)+1} & =\\sqrt{8+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338888 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=f(0)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{({(f(x))}^{2}-3)}^{2} & ={({2}^{2}-3)}^{2}={1}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338889 :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that $\\lim_{x\\to 4}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 4}{({x}^{2}-6f(x))}^{2} & ={({4}^{2}-6(2))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(16-12)}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338890 :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=-2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{3}-x, & x<-2 \\\\ 6, & x=-2 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+x, & x>-2\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186338890 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- $f(-2)=6,$ so $f(-2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}({x}^{3}-x) \\\\  & =(-2)^{3}-(-2) \\\\  & =-8+2 \\\\  & =-6, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(-{x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =-(-2)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =-4-2 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=-6$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=-6,$ while $f(-2)=6.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-2$ because $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)\\ne f(-2).$"] [17592186338891 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1.$\n\n- $f(-1)=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{2} \\\\  & =1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1,$ while $f(-1)=2.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1).$"] [17592186338892 :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}-2x+3, & x<2 \\\\ 2x-3, & x\\ge 2\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186338892 :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2)=2(2)-3=1,$ so $f(2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-2x+3) \\\\  & =-2(2)+3 \\\\  & =-1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(2x-3) \\\\  & =2(2)-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. Therefore, $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist. $\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because $\\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist."] [17592186338909 :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =\\begin{cases}3x, & x\\ne 4 \\\\ 13, & x=4,\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186338909 :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(4)=13$ exists$\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{4}^{-}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{4}^{-}}3x=12, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}g(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}3x=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)$ is true and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 4}g(x)=12.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is $\\lim_{x\\to 4}g(x)=12,$ while the value of the function is $g(4)=13.$ So, statement $\\lim_{x\\to 4}g(x)=g(4)$ is false and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 4}g(x)\\ne g(4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=4,$ and statement “$g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4$” is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)$ and “$g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4$” are true."] [17592186338910 :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{cases}4x, & x<2 \\\\ 4, & x=2 \\\\ {x}^{2}, & x>2\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements is true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186338910 :question/worked-solution "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(2)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, statement “$f(2)$ exists” is true.\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}(4x)=8, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}{x}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that statement $\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)$ is false and\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the second condition failed, $x=2$ is not a removable discontinuity. So, statement “$f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=2$” is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement “$f(2)$ exists” is true."] [17592186339089 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{100x}{{x}^{2}-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[100x]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-1]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{100}{2x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186339090 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{1/x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}} & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{e}^{1/x}]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{{e}^{1/x}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{{e}^{1/x}\\cdot {x}^{-2}} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{x}{{e}^{1/x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186339091 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}\\cancel{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}}{\\cancel{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}} \\\\  & =4\\lim_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186339092 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{-3}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186339093 :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are $\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty $ and $\\lim_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{7}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6{x}^{-7}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186339763 :answer/value "q(x)=\\begin{cases}2x,\\, & x<1 \\\\ x+2,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{cases}"] [17592186341907 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=f(-1)=-2.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\,\\frac{1}{3f(x)-10x} & =\\frac{1}{3\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)-10\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(-2)-10(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186341918 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=-5{x}^{-2},$ the lower bound is $a=-4,$ and the upper bound is $b=-2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{-2-(-4)}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(-4+j\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186341919 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}f({x}_{m})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{m}=a+mΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{6}ln⁡(4x-3)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=ln⁡(4x-3),$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-1}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}ln⁡(4x-3)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}f({x}_{m})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[f(1+m\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{5m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[ln⁡(4(1+\\frac{5m}{n})-3)\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{m=1}^{n}[ln⁡(1+\\frac{20m}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186341933 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{3}-1, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 1, & x=0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186341933 :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement “It is continuous and differentiable at $x=0$” is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}({x}^{3}-1)={0}^{3}-1=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(0)=1,\n$$\n\n \nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$ Therefore, it's not differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement “It is **not** continuous at $x=0$ but it is differentiable at $x=0$” is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement “It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-1,1)$” is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only statement “It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-1,1)$” is true."] [17592186341934 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}x+1, & x<1 \\\\ 2, & x\\ge 1\\end{cases}\\,\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186341934 :question/worked-solution "- Statement “It is continuous at $x=1$” and “It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$” are true. Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly at  $x=1.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(x+1)=1+1=2 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}2=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=f(1)=2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is  continuous at $x=1$ . However, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=1\\,$  if  $\\,x<1$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>1$\n\n- Statement “It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$” is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statements “It is continuous at $x=1$” and “It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$” are true."] [17592186341972 :question/worked-solution "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c|c}x & (-\\infty ,0) & 0 & (0,3) & 3 & (3,\\infty ) \\\\ \\hline f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ \\hline {f}^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,3).$"] [17592186341990 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[-1,1].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}({2}^{-x})={2}^{0}=1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$ In this interval, $f(x)={2}^{-x}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, the interval $[-1,0)$, is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$ only."] [17592186341991 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- First, consider $[0,2].$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Now, consider the interval $[-1,0].$ In this interval, $f(x)=x-1$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,0].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, in the interval $[-1,2]$, we also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,2]$ and therefore  the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[-1,0]$ only."] [17592186341993 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ \\,\\,2, & x=0.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186341993 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- First, let's consider $[-1,1].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2-x}{{2}^{x}+1}=\\frac{2-0}{{2}^{0}+1}=1\\ne f(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,-1].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0,$ which is not in the interval  $[-2,-1].$  Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[-2,-1],$  and the extreme value theorem is also applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[-2,-1]$ only."] [17592186341994 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\,\\,2{x}^{3}-1, & x<1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡(x-1), & x\\ge 1.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186341994 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-1,2].$  We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}(2{x}^{3}-1)=2(1)^{3}-1=1, \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to 1}{sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)={sin}^{2}⁡(1-1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(1,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[2,\\pi ].$ In this interval, $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(x-1)$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,\\pi ].$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[2,\\pi ]$ only."] [17592186342003 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals. \n\n- Let first consider the interval  $[-2,2].$ This is a  closed interval, but we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{2}^{x-1}-1}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ has an essential discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-1,1]$, and the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-3,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable in this case.\n\n- Finally consider  $[1.5,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=1,$ which is not in the interval  $[1.5,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval  $[1.5,2]$, and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[1.5,2]$ only."] [17592186342007 :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}\\right)=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4x}\\right) & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186342010 :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{{\\theta }^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right)}{{\\theta }^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }]}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡0}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\theta }^{2}}\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186342011 :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ÷\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }÷\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }]}^{-1} \\\\  & ={1}^{-1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186344992 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x-y).\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when $4-x-y$  is negative or zero. However, at the point $(1,2)$,  the expression  $4-x-y$ is positive, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,2)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x-y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-1-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186344993 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So,  we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,4)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2,4)}\\sqrt[3]{xy}=\\sqrt{(-2)\\cdot 4}=\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186344994 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz}=\\frac{1\\cdot {2}^{2}-1\\cdot {2}^{2}}{1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{(x-y){z}^{2}}{(y-x)z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}-\\frac{(x-y){z}^{2}}{(x-y)z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}(-z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1,2)}(-z) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"] [17592186345000 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+y)^{2}}{(y-x)(y+x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x+y)}{(y-x)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+y}{y-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+y}{y-x} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"] [17592186345001 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{1+2}}{3-1-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2} & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{y+2})^{2}}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{x-y-2}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{1+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}.$"] [17592186345002 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1}-1}{1-{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186345003 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=\\frac{{1}^{2}-1}{\\sqrt{1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y} & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{-(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{x-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{1}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"] [17592186345004 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{2x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot 0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $2$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186345005 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0\\cdot {0}^{3}}{{0}^{6}+8\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{4}+8)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+8} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{3}}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{9{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"] [17592186348055 :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{2{x}^{2}+x} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(2+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(x+0)}{(2+0)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186348056 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{2}+5}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{5}{x})}{\\left(\\frac{2x}{x}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x+\\frac{5}{x})}{2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x+0)}{2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }2x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [17592186348057 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{5}+1}{4{x}^{3}-x+1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}+0)}{(4-0+0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [17592186348061 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x){e}^{-2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x){e}^{-2x}) & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186348062 :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)} & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}}{(-7{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }-\\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186348367 :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}} & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\sqrt{1-z}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-2\\sqrt{1-z}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{1-0}-\\sqrt{1-a}) \\\\  & =-2+2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{1-a} \\\\  & =-2+2\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent."] [17592186348399 :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d4a0a08513d817fe841f1e45e5d363d6e64241837dea71f38aef452f86b08a7.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{cases}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186348399 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles $R=[0,1]\\times [-1,1]$ and $R=[0,2]\\times [0,1]$ do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$ only."] [17592186348412 :question/problem "![](images/d9/d92fdb93f7564e1c205ab8603945eb6704c03db92c46130810e2b7cbce9a0e20.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}+xy & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186348412 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}+xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,3]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$” only."] [17592186348413 :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{2}-4$ and the $x$-axis. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}3x-y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186348413 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/a7/a7af801fa9975dda6ce3b12b2cde2c328721cd5e0affd16f1a14ab26cfa21bdd.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}(3x-y), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statements “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [-4,1]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-4,4]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statements “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-4,0]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [-4,1]$”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(3x-y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-4,0]$” only."] [17592186351279 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}3{y}^{2},\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<9,\\,-1<y<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{xy}},\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<9,\\,1<y<4,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$"] [17592186351298 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is convergent” is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }({-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(-{e}^{-{a}^{2}}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(-{e}^{0}+{e}^{-{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is false. The integral in statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1-1=0.\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is convergent” is true."] [17592186351451 :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-2,3):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,3]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to (-2)^{+}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to (-2)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+8}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186351456 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{u}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u||}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2}{a}| \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent."] [17592186351457 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=(-1)^{n+1}{4}^{n}{x}^{n+3}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{4}^{n+1}{x}^{n+4}}{(-1)^{n}{4}^{n}{x}^{n+3}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|-4x| \\\\  & =4|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n4|x|<1\\,⇒\\,|x|<\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{4}.$"] [17592186351458 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{{5}^{2(n+1)}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2(n+1)}}{{5}^{2(n+2)}}\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{2(n+1)}}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1){x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|{x}^{2}|}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}<25\\,⇒\\,|x|<\\sqrt{25}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=5.$"] [17592186351460 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{{2}^{n}(2n)!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n+3}}{{2}^{n+1}(2n+2)!}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n}(2n)!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1){x}^{2}}{2(2n+2)(2n+1)}| \\\\  & =|-{x}^{2}|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{2(2n+2)(2n+1)} \\\\  & =|{x}^{2}|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$"] [17592186354528 :question/problem "![](images/47/4772b690cb85fcb4dffd27ac4ba56344307ca106e927b9945144370b266d2be9.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{cases}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186354528 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$ and $R=[-1,0]\\times [0,1]$ do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$ only."] [17592186354555 :question/problem "Compute $P(X>6)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}3{e}^{-3x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186357405 :question/problem "Find $L{f(t)}$ for the piecewise function defined by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<1 \\\\ -7,\\hspace{1em} & 1\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ 0,\\hspace{1em} & t>3.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186357405 :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{cases}0\\hspace{1em} & \\text{for}\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}t<1 \\\\ -7\\hspace{1em} & \\text{for}\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}1\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ 0\\hspace{1em} & \\text{for}\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}t>3\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t={\\int }_{1}^{3}(-7){e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate this definite integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(-7){e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{7}{s}{e}^{-st}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{s}({e}^{-3s}-{e}^{-s})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{7}{s}({e}^{-3s}-{e}^{-s}).\n$$"] [17592186360758 :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.64),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{(0.36)}^{x-1}(0.64), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & ={(0.36)}^{0}(0.64)+{(0.36)}^{1}(0.64)+{(0.36)}^{2}(0.64)+{(0.36)}^{3}(0.64) \\\\  & ≈0.9832.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186360763 :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{(0.3)}^{x-1}(0.7), & x=1,2,3… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X>3.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(0.3)}^{0}(0.7)+{(0.3)}^{1}(0.7)+{(0.3)}^{2}(0.7) \\\\  & =0.973.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-0.973 \\\\  & =0.027.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186360764 :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.4),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{(0.6)}^{x-1}(0.4), & x=1,2,3… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 4.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X<4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(0.6)}^{0}(0.4)+{(0.6)}^{1}(0.4)+{(0.6)}^{2}(0.4) \\\\  & =0.784.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X<4) \\\\  & =1-0.784 \\\\  & =0.216.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362334 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}={x}^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|x| \\\\  & =|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x|<1.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=1.$"] [17592186362345 :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n+3}}{(2n+3)!}\\cdot \\frac{(2n+1)!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}| \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1){x}^{2}}{(2n+3)(2n+2)}| \\\\  & =|-{x}^{2}|\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n+3)(2n+2)} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty ,$ and the interval of convergence is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [17592186362355 :question/worked-solution "The graph $y={x}^{12}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{12}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $8$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(8{x}^{12})=\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{12})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362357 :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit. In this case, we raise the main term of the base polynomial to the given power.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }({t}^{2}+1)^{4} & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }({t}^{2})^{4} \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{8} \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362398 :question/problem "Find the limit at $x=-1$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}-\\sqrt[3]{x}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<-1 \\\\ -2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x=-1 \\\\ x+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>-1.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186362398 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}-\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{-1} \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(x+2) \\\\  & =-1+2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $1,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.\n$$"] [17592186362399 :question/problem "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -1 \\\\ x+c\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that $\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)=1.$ What is the value of the constant $c?$"] [17592186362399 :question/worked-solution "In order for $\\lim_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ to be equal to $1,$ the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal to $1.$\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(-x) \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+c.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(x+c) \\\\  & =-1+c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 1 & =-1+c \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362400 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}-x & \\text{ for }\\,x<-2 \\\\ x & \\text{ for }\\,x>3.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186362400 :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=-2$ from the left, we have $x<-2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}(-x) \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the left-sided limit $\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in [-2,3].$ \n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186362401 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2} & \\text{ for }\\,x<-1 \\\\ 3 & \\text{ for }\\,x>1.\\end{cases}\n$$"] [17592186362401 :question/worked-solution "Approaching $x=-1$ from the left, we have $x<-1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, approaching $x=1$ from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}3=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362630 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=3$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{\\sqrt{x+4}-\\sqrt{7}} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}-3(3)}{\\sqrt{3+4}-\\sqrt{7}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{\\sqrt{x+4}-\\sqrt{7}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x(x-3)}{\\sqrt{x+4}-\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7})}{(\\sqrt{x+4}-\\sqrt{7})(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7})}{(\\sqrt{x+4})^{2}-(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7})}{x+4-7} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x(x-3)(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7})}{x-3} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 3}x(\\sqrt{x+4}+\\sqrt{7}) \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{3+4}+\\sqrt{7}) \\\\  & =3(2\\sqrt{7}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362928 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362935 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-20n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-20} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-20} \\\\  & ={e}^{-20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{20}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362948 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-n\\theta } & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-\\theta } \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{-\\theta } \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186362955 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{y}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{-4n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{n}]}^{-4} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{y}{n})}^{n}]}^{-4} \\\\  & ={({e}^{y})}^{-4} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{4y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186363017 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{u}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{u}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nHence, with $u=2y$, we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{2y}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n/2} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{2y}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={({e}^{2y})}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2y/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186363405 :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f955ee719a4e69f0d09c678f0a74023f137efd7b5ae36d49b98706156384173.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}x+\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186363692 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-7}{x-49} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{49}-7}{49-49}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-7}{x-49} & =\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-7)(\\sqrt{x}+7)}{(x-49)(\\sqrt{x}+7)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-{7}^{2}}{(x-49)(\\sqrt{x}+7)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{x-49}{(x-49)(\\sqrt{x}+7)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{\\cancel{x-49}}{\\cancel{(x-49)}(\\sqrt{x}+7)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 49}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{49}+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7+7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186363695 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n(x+y)} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2(x+y)} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2(x+y)} \\\\  & ={e}^{2(x+y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186363698 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{x}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{3n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{3} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{3} \\\\  & ={({e}^{x})}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186363941 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{9n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{9} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{9} \\\\  & ={e}^{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186363944 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/5} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/5} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/5} \\\\  & ={e}^{1/5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[5]{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186363947 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{7nyz} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{7yz} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{7yz} \\\\  & ={e}^{7yz}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186363950 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{x}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{-1} \\\\  & ={({e}^{x})}^{-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186363953 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -3}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x+22}-4}{x+3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(-3)+22}-4}{-3+3}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to -3}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x+22}-4}{x+3} & =\\lim_{x\\to -3}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x+22}-4)(\\sqrt{2x+22}+4)}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{2x+22}+4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -3}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2x+22})}^{2}-{4}^{2}}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{2x+22}+4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -3}\\frac{2x+22-16}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{2x+22}+4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -3}\\frac{2x+6}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{2x+22}+4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -3}\\frac{2(x+3)}{(x+3)(\\sqrt{2x+22}+4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -3}\\frac{2\\cancel{(x+3)}}{\\cancel{(x+3)}(\\sqrt{2x+22}+4)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to -3}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x+22}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2(-3)+22}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{16}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186364199 :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-3}{x-8} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{8+1}-3}{8-8}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+1}-3}{x-8} & =\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+3)}{(x-8)(\\sqrt{x+1}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x+1})}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{(x-8)(\\sqrt{x+1}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{x+1-9}{(x-8)(\\sqrt{x+1}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{x-8}{(x-8)(\\sqrt{x+1}+3)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+1}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8+1}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
